# Citation metadata for IQ-TREE3-GPU (Citation File Format 1.2.0).
# Validate with: cffconvert --validate
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use IQ-TREE3-GPU, please cite the manuscript under preferred-citation together with the
  IQ-TREE 3 article it builds on (Wong et al. 2026, doi:10.1093/molbev/msag117), listed under
  references. The benchmark data (source genomes, alignments, run record) are archived on Zenodo
  under the DOI listed in identifiers.
title: "IQ-TREE3-GPU: GPU-accelerated maximum-likelihood phylogenetic tree inference for complex amino acid substitution models"
abstract: >-
  Maximum-likelihood (ML) phylogenetic inference requires repeated computationally intensive
  likelihood calculations, particularly for genome-scale amino-acid alignments and complex
  evolutionary models. Although graphics processing units (GPUs) can accelerate these calculations,
  achieving substantial end-to-end acceleration within established phylogenetic inference workflows
  remains challenging. Here, we introduce IQ-TREE3-GPU, a CUDA likelihood backend integrated directly
  into IQ-TREE 3 that accelerates Felsenstein's pruning algorithm while retaining its existing
  model-selection, tree-search and branch-support procedures. We evaluated IQ-TREE3-GPU using
  computationally demanding phylogenomic datasets constructed from 435 host-associated bacterial
  metagenome-assembled genomes spanning 14 genera and 23 host species, alongside complementary
  simulated nucleotide datasets. Across 182 paired CPU-GPU analyses, IQ-TREE3-GPU recovered identical
  maximum-likelihood tree topologies, with all final log-likelihood differences within a predefined
  numerical tolerance. For empirical amino-acid phylogenetic inference with ModelFinder, GPU execution
  achieved a median 3.73-fold end-to-end speedup against a 20-thread CPU baseline, reducing the
  largest analysis from 17.6 to 3.4 hours. Notably, GPU acceleration increased with tree complexity,
  demonstrating greater benefits for computationally demanding phylogenetic analyses. GPU execution
  also reduced host processor-time consumption by a median factor of 37.0, with consistent amino-acid
  acceleration across the tested NVIDIA GPU configurations. Together, these findings establish
  IQ-TREE3-GPU as a practical tool for accelerating computationally intensive amino-acid phylogenetic
  inference, with increasing benefits as computational workload grows.
